{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>[And the people saw.</b> According to its plain sense, even regarding something that is not seen but is known to be so, the language of seeing applies.]" "<b>And the people assembled.</b> Every assembly after which comes על is for disrepute. And if after it comes אל, it is for praise — as in 'ויקהלו אל המלך שלמה' (and they assembled to King Solomon). 'Arise, make us elohim' — according to the plain sense, heaven forbid that Aaron should make idolatry, for he was holy to HaShem and a prophet to Israel, and many commandments were given through him together with Moses his brother. And if he made an idol, how would he escape being killed by Moses, and how afterward could he atone for Israel, he and his seed, to the end of all generations? Furthermore, we do not find that Scripture mentions any injustice about him, only the sin of Merivah. And all the upright of heart acknowledge that HaShem would not choose a messenger whose end is to worship idolatry. Also Israel did not ask and did not intend idolatry. But Moses did not tell them when he would come down, nor did he know, for HaShem said to him: 'Go up to Me to the mountain and be there, and I will give you the tablets of the covenant' — and the time He did not tell him. And when they saw he delayed so long, they thought he had died, for a human being has no strength to remain so long without eating. And the meaning of <b>elohim</b> is judge and ruler and leader — as in 'See, I have made you elohim to Pharaoh.' And the reason they gave proves this, for they said 'for this Moses, the man' — meaning, in place of Moses they sought to set it up. And what David said, 'וימירו את כבודם' (and they exchanged their glory) — HaShem forbid — means they exchanged Moses, who was their glory, for through him HaShem performed many miracles for them, 'וישתחוו למסכה' (and bowed down to the molten image) — to honor it, not for the purpose of idolatry. Another interpretation: 'make us elohim' — that the Shekhinah of HaShem would dwell within its body." "<b>Which shall go before us.</b> For the glory of HaShem. And they associated Aaron with it, and in this they erred, for they thought he could lead them, since they held Aaron to be accustomed to miracles." "<b>For this Moses, the man.</b> It is the language of pointing, such that even regarding something not present before the speaker they say about it 'this' — for example, 'ראה זה מצאתי אמרה קהלת' (see, this I have found, says Kohelet), and many more."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:01:32.023163154Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}